# The native shell's build artefacts (issue #36).
#
# Scoped to this directory on purpose. The repo root's .gitignore describes a
# no-build static site and should keep describing one.

# Capacitor's dependencies. The reason this whole directory exists is to keep
# these out of the repo root.
node_modules/

# THE webDir. Generated by ../tools/mobile/prepare-webdir.mjs on every build,
# and it is a COPY of index.html / app.js / styles.css / player/ / the runtime
# data subset. Committing it would create the second copy of the player that the
# prepare script's header exists to prevent: the site would get a fix and the
# committed copy would not.
www/

# ── The generated native projects: BOTH IGNORED IN FULL ──────────────────────
#
# This block used to say the opposite — that `mobile/ios/` and `mobile/android/`
# are NOT ignored, because Capacitor's own guidance is to commit them. Both
# platforms have since been generated, and the practice the repo actually settled
# on is the other one, so the rule now matches it. Written down rather than
# quietly flipped, because the old argument was not silly.
#
# WHAT SETTLED IT — the same call, twice, independently:
#   - iOS. PR #213 (on #220) builds the shell on a macOS runner by running
#     `npm run add:ios` in the job and building what it generates. `mobile/ios/`
#     is not in the repo and the workflow does not want it to be.
#   - Android. Issue #37 built `mobile/android/` on Windows and got both APKs
#     (`docs/android-shell-build.md`). Committing it was rejected for the same
#     reason: ~100 generated files, no reviewer, and this directory AUTO-MERGES
#     with no post-merge review window.
#
# So the generate step must be reproducible from `mobile/package.json` alone, and
# it is: `npm install && npm run add:android` (or `add:ios`) needs nothing that is
# not committed. `capacitor.config.json` — the only file that carries a DECISION
# (the app id, the platform paths) — is committed, and is pinned by
# `tools/mobile/shell-invariants.test.mjs`.
#
# THE ONE THING THIS COSTS, stated plainly because it is a real loss. The Android
# `res/xml/config.xml` was deliberately left un-ignored so the `KeepRunning`
# footgun could be parsed by a test — an ignored file cannot be checked. Ignoring
# the platform means CI never sees that file. The vector it defended against goes
# away with it: `KeepRunning="false"` can no longer be committed, because the file
# it would live in can no longer be committed. `cap add` regenerates it from
# Capacitor's template on every machine, and the template sets no preference at
# all. The guard is now a check on the machine that generated the platform —
# which is the machine that builds the APK, so it still runs where it matters.
# The committed half of that guard (the parsed `capacitor.config.json`, at any
# depth, case-insensitively) is untouched and still runs in CI.
ios/
android/

# What the platforms produce, kept for anyone who un-ignores a platform above.
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
*.iml
*.apk
*.aab
